{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789253667,"nonce":"azxXf2ErAVTY3/TSuOZTbj4kDh4=","title":"Python: argument and statement forms still rejected","message":"Forms the lowerer rejects or mis-handles that appear in ordinary code:\n\n1. `*args` and `**kwargs` in a def, and `f(*xs)`, `f(**d)` at a call.\n   `*args` is a tuple parameter; `**kwargs` is a dict; the TypedAST\n   already has ParameterKind::Rest.\n2. Starred unpacking in assignment: `a, *rest = xs`, `first, *_, last`.\n3. Chained assignment `a = b = 0` and unpacking through attributes and\n   subscripts: `self.x, self.y = pt`, `xs[i], xs[j] = xs[j], xs[i]`.\n4. Loop `else` on `for` and `while`.\n5. The walrus `:=`.\n6. Slice assignment and deletion: `xs[1:3] = [...]`, `del xs[1:3]`,\n   `del d[k]` on dicts is done.\n7. Keyword arguments passed through a function value or a dynamic\n   method call; today only direct calls to known defs take them.\n8. Decorators: `@f` on a def is `name = f(name)`; needs f1b2928 gap 2\n   for builtins and functools. `@dataclass` is the common one and is\n   better handled by the class collector than by a call, together\n   with `@staticmethod`, `@classmethod`, `@property` from d3acf39.\n\nc0bf352 lists most of these as planned desugarings; this issue tracks\nthem as open work.","files":null}]}